From 23b9945ef8d39bbf1bebf80a9d3dc1a7e2aaba0f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=E6=99=9A=E5=87=9DW4nN1ng?= <wn2276519232@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 17:24:26 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] Add a friend status light dashboard

Picking a friend from the new Charts tab now shows how their four status
lights divide up over time and when each one was on, down to the minute.

Reading a real database turned up two things that would have made the
numbers wrong. First, a feed_status row whose status equals previous_status
is not a duplicate: 169 of 796 rows record an edit to the custom text
attached to a light, so discarding them as noise would have silently
dropped a fifth of the real events. They are kept and reported as text
edits, and they deliberately do not cut the current light's interval short.
Second, carrying a light forward across an unobserved stretch does not work:
when two observations are under an hour apart the light differs 92% of the
time, so those stretches stay unknown and only the single case with a
recorded previous_status is filled in, flagged as an estimate.

The percentages cover only time a light was actually recorded; offline,
online-without-a-record and VRCX-not-running are listed separately so the
shares are never diluted by guesswork. Offline events repeat themselves and
lag behind status changes, so a status change is treated as proof of being
online, and neighbouring intervals in the same state are merged.

feed_status also gains the (user_id, created_at) index the other feed
tables already had.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
